ECOSYSTEM ATTRIBUTION
=====================

QuietBooks is built on patterns established by the Midnight Foundation and the
wider ecosystem. Where a file adapts someone else's work closely, its header
says so and carries the original licence. This file records the debts that are
architectural rather than line-by-line.

Every Compact circuit, every witness implementation and every line of the
application is original to this project. Compact is neither Solidity nor Leo,
so nothing could be copied even where we wanted to: what was borrowed is
design, and it is listed here so a reader can go and check the originals.


Midnight Foundation (https://github.com/midnightntwrk)
------------------------------------------------------

  midnightntwrk/example-bboard                          Apache-2.0
      The project layout (contract / api / cli / ui), the Lace DApp Connector
      wiring, and the provider assembly in the browser: FetchZkConfigProvider,
      httpClientProofProvider, indexerPublicDataProvider, and the balanceTx /
      submitTx adapters. Offered by Midnight as a template, and used as one.

  midnightntwrk/example-zkloan                          Apache-2.0
      The identity model. Every party key in QuietBooks is a domain-separated
      hash of a witness-held secret, and `ownPublicKey()` is never used for
      authorisation. That rule, and the PIN-rotation pattern that lets a party
      shed a linkable history, are taken from this example.

  midnightntwrk/example-counter                         Apache-2.0
      The minimal compile target used to verify a toolchain installation. Its
      contract source is reproduced verbatim at `e2e/counter-src/counter.compact`
      and deployed by `e2e/src/control.ts`: when our own deploy failed at
      submission, deploying a one-circuit contract through the identical wallet
      and providers was what separated a fault in our contract from a fault in
      the environment.

  midnightntwrk/midnight-local-dev                      Apache-2.0
      The local network composition in `localnet/standalone.yml`, the image
      versions that are known to work together, and the wallet sync, funding and
      DUST-registration sequence reproduced in `e2e/src/wallet.ts`. The genesis
      seed used by the end-to-end run is documented there.

  midnightntwrk/midnight-docs                           Apache-2.0
      The compatibility matrix that pins every version this project uses.


OpenZeppelin (https://github.com/OpenZeppelin)
----------------------------------------------

  OpenZeppelin/compact-contracts                        MIT
      The shielded custody model, twice over. `ShieldedTreasury` and
      `ShieldedTreasuryStateless` document, in their own headers, that a
      contract holding a shielded coin necessarily publishes its value.
      `ForwarderShielded._deposit` shows the way around it: `receiveShielded`
      followed by `sendImmediateShielded` in one call routes a payment without
      ever holding it, so the balance never moves and the value stays hidden.
      `settleWithNote` is that pattern, applied to an invoice. Between them
      these shaped QuietBooks' central design decision: the private
      settlement path does not take custody, and the escrow path does and says
      so. The `Ownable` witness-derived account identity and the per-module
      initialisation flag are also modelled on this library.


Independent ecosystem projects
------------------------------

  DpacJones/selkie-usdm-escrow                          MIT
      Copyright (c) 2026 Dennis Zarelli (Atlantis)
      The commitment and claim discipline: compute the commitment in-circuit
      from the witness rather than accepting it as an argument; bind a second
      authority commitment to the record it authorises; keep a settled map
      entry as its own nullifier instead of maintaining a separate set; and
      reject an all-zero secret at the witness boundary, because the hiding
      property rests entirely on it. Selkie's own header names the gap its
      design leaves .. a cancel with no deadline .. and QuietBooks closes it.

  robertocarlous/Shadow-Payroll                         Apache-2.0
      The continuous-integration shape, including pinning the Compact toolchain
      to an exact version rather than tracking the latest, and the practice of
      making a deadline testable by allowing the clock to be supplied.

  PhiBao/candor                                         Apache-2.0
      The epoch-scoped nullifier, where the epoch is read from ledger state so a
      caller cannot misrepresent which period they are acting in.

  WHXisWH/alpaca-invoice                                MIT
      The product design for private invoicing: the invoice lifecycle, per-field
      commitments, and above all the selective-disclosure audit model .. an
      authorisation naming an auditor, a scope restricted to specific fields,
      and an expiry, recorded on chain while the data itself travels out of
      band. QuietBooks' audit envelope is a reimplementation of that idea for
      Compact. No Leo source was copied; it does not run on Midnight.

  NethermindEth/stellar-private-payments                Apache-2.0
      The shape of a portable disclosure receipt: context fields bound into the
      proof by a hash, so that altering any of them invalidates the receipt
      while leaving the cryptography intact. Read as a design reference only.

  sablier-labs/evm-monorepo                             BUSL-1.1
      Read, not used. The linear unlock formula in `LockupMath.sol` was studied
      while designing time-gated release. No code was taken: the licence does
      not permit it, and the arithmetic is short enough to state independently.


